  "rewrites": {
   "14": {
    "original": "I think the upside is clearer now than when Apple first introduced this generation of the iPhone, and I really love a lot of the missing parts, but the harsh reality is that while full multitasking would address a lot of the issues and improve my level of satisfaction (which shows more cracks as each month goes by), it would not make it a work device for me.",
    "rewrite": "I believe the advantages are more apparent now compared to when Apple debuted this iteration of the iPhone, and I appreciate many of the missing features, but the grim truth remains that although embracing full multitasking would resolve numerous problems and enhance my degree of contentment (which exhibits more flaws as time passes), it still wouldn't convert it into a professional device for me."
   },
   "2": {
    "original": "I also haven't seen evidence that a larger screen would enable more battery life (does anybody have info?) - the physics of whichever size screen I'm holding is the limiting factor - it takes the same amount of energy to display pixels regardless of how many there are.",
    "rewrite": "Similarly, I haven't found evidence that a bigger screen allows for better battery life; rather, the size of the screen I'm currently holding determines the energy usage, as displaying pixels consumes the same amount of energy irrespective of their quantity."
   },
   "8": {
    "original": "But a 4.75-inch Retina is easy to sell; maybe Apple will either do it the next upgrade cycle or extend its cycle to let the iPhone die down, make iOS even more prominent, and really push it at the holidays next year.",
    "rewrite": "But an easy-to-advertise 4.75-inch Retina display might prompt Apple to either include it in the next model update or maintain the current cycle to allow the iPhone to become less trendy, emphasizing iOS significantly, and then aggressively pushing it during the holiday season next year."
   },
   "12": {
    "original": "Has anyone noticed if they've been able to cobble a 4:3 aspect ratio together from iTunes, YouTube, websites, apps, and ebooks after downloading them?",
    "rewrite": "Has anyone observed if they can assemble a 4:3 aspect ratio from downloads of content in iTunes, YouTube, websites, apps, or ebooks?"
   },
   "3": {
    "original": "I suspect phones we're are holding will be getting an extra 50th of an inch each time - adding 0.5-1.0 inch would not be intolerable.",
