  "final_span": "phone if every phone maxed out at 4.3-4.5 inches. I'm willing to spend more for the additional screen space on my iPhone \u2014 it wouldn't exceed ~$150. Similarly, I haven't found evidence that a bigger screen allows for better battery life; rather, the size of the screen I'm currently holding determines the energy usage, as displaying pixels consumes the same amount of energy irrespective of their quantity.\nI suspect that each phone will grow by an additional 50th of an inch\u2014adding 0.5 to 1.0 inch would not be considered excessive.\nIs it possible that Apple (and others) might release a 4.75-inch Retina phone? I believe that would displease some iPhone users. This would introduce the risk of feature envy. To be certain, I am actually not certain about feature envy. But an easy-to-advertise 4.75-inch Retina display might prompt Apple to either include it in the next model update or maintain the current cycle to allow the iPhone to become less trendy, emphasizing iOS significantly, and then aggressively pushing it during the holiday season next year. I reckon that both scenarios could be incorrect or correct.\nServices such as Google or others I've interacted with back then recognize 4:3 aspect ratio. I would dislike losing 4 inches in width, and it appears the constraint is the film's 4:3 aspect ratio. Has anyone observed if they can assemble a 4:3 aspect ratio from downloads of content in iTunes, YouTube, websites, apps, or ebooks? This idea has been on my mind a great deal this week, as I would really like to work effectively with the iPad.\nI believe the advantages are more apparent now compared to when Apple debuted this iteration of the iPhone, and I appreciate many of the missing features, but the grim truth remains that although embracing full multitasking would resolve numerous problems and enhance my degree of contentment (which exhibits more flaws as time passes), it still wouldn't convert it into a professional device for me. The graphics chip is the biggest limiting factor for doing anything beyond web browser (or heavy text editing), and not",
